// ==== 47-music-1-engine.js
// =====================================================================================
// THE INSTRUMENTS' SOUND, BY PHYSICS (owner, 15 Oct 2026: "create a page like this in game to practice that actual works then based on real
// physics math etc"; "a music store that has every instrument we can replicate"): no recordings. One AudioWorklet processor, 'imx', runs one
// instrument on the browser's audio thread, sample by sample; each instrument played (yours, and each other player's within earshot) is a node
// of it, made when it's first heard and let go after a while silent. Four families, each the physics of the real thing:
//  PLUCKED STRINGS (guitars, bass, ukulele, banjo, mandolin, harp): each string a travelling wave in a delay line as long as the string's sound
//    takes to go round at its pitch (tuned to a fraction of a sample by an allpass: the pitch exact), losing a little each round trip, the high
//    partials first (a one-pole at the bridge), a touch of stiffness (an allpass: wound strings' partials a hair sharp); a pluck sets the
//    string's shape, a triangle peaked where it's pulled, rounder from flesh, sharper from a pick or nail (47-play-3b's guitar, the same
//    waveguide). The strings drive the body at the bridge: its resonances (the air in it, the top's and back's modes) ring with them and feed
//    a little back into every string (the open ones sing in sympathy). An electric's sound is its pickup's instead: the string seen from a
//    point along it (a comb: the wave and its reflection), through the amp's valves (soft clipping) and its speaker (a band, 80 Hz to 5 kHz).
//    A mandolin's string is a pair, a few cents apart.
//  BOWED STRINGS (violin, cello): the bow's stick and slip (the McIntyre, Schumacher and Woodhouse model): the string's two parts, nut side
//    and bridge side of the bow, as two delay lines; where the bow meets the string the friction curve (high while they move together, falling
//    off as they slip) decides how much of the bow's speed the string takes; the bridge's loss, the body's modes (the violin's A0, the B1 pair,
//    the bridge hill near 2.5 kHz). Faster bow, louder; harder, brighter and, past a point, scratchy, as a real one.
//  BLOWN PIPES (flute, recorder): the jet over the edge and the air column (Cook's waveguide flute): breath pressure with its noise, the jet's
//    travel time, the jet's non-linearity (x^3 - x, clipped), the bore as a delay line, the open end's loss. Blow harder and it overblows
//    to the octave, as a real flute does.
//  STRUCK THINGS (drums, cymbals, bars, bells, tines, pans): modal synthesis: each struck body rings in its own modes, each a damped
//    sinusoid (a two-pole resonator) at its real ratio to the first (a stretched membrane: Bessel's zeros; a free bar: 1 : 2.756 : 5.404;
//    a marimba's undercut bar: 1 : 4 : 10; a xylophone's: 1 : 3 : 6; a clamped tine: 1 : 6.27 : 17.55; a tube: 1 : 2.76 : 5.40 : 8.93 ...),
//    each as loud as the mallet's contact time lets it be (a half-sine pulse's spectrum: soft mallets dull, hard ones bright) and as the
//    point struck lets it be (a membrane's mode shapes: the middle of a drum dull, nearer the rim more ring). A snare's wires, a cymbal's
//    wash: noise through the shell's band, its own decay.
// Each node posts back its strings' (or bars') motion fifteen times a second for the practice view to draw (IMX.motion). Silent a second
// and nothing due, a node rests (no work) till the next note. What it costs: on the audio thread only, one node per instrument sounding.
// =====================================================================================
